<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-parallel-vectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Parallel Vectors</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.6 Vectors II
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine parallelism of vectors in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Parallel vectors: a = kb; testing vectors for parallelism.
         Suggested activity: Learners test pairs of vectors for parallelism by checking whether one is a scalar multiple of the other.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Two vectors with a slider for k showing b = ka; the vectors stay parallel as k changes and reverse direction when k < 0. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core 2.6 (e): parallel vectors
         Activity: as planning notes (test pairs of vectors for a common scalar multiple)
         JSXGraph: fig-parallel-vectors-interactive (assets/jsxgraph/parallel-vectors.js), as planning notes; drawn in two dimensions so the direction is easy to see
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/12-vectors-ii/05-parallel-vectors/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> are parallel when <m>\mathbf{a} = k\mathbf{b}</m>; the sign of <m>k</m> gives the direction; <m>|\mathbf{a}| = |k||\mathbf{b}|</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> test pairs of vectors for parallelism; find <m>k</m> and unknown components; find a parallel vector of a given magnitude.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> decide whether pipes or edges are parallel.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners discover the scalar-multiple test for parallel vectors, including vectors in opposite directions.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Work in pairs. After part (c), ask: <q>Is it enough that two of the components are in the same ratio?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-scalar-multiples">
        <title>Scalar Multiples</title>
        <statement>
            <p>For each pair, decide whether the second vector is a number times the first. If it is, find the number.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 12 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The second is <m>3</m> times the first: parallel, same direction.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \\ -6 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ -1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The second is <m>-\frac{1}{2}</m> times the first: parallel, opposite direction.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The ratios are <m>2</m>, <m>2</m> and <m>\frac{5}{2}</m>: not all the same, so not parallel.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 3 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -6 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m></p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The second is <m>-2</m> times the first (and <m>-2 \times 0 = 0</m>): parallel, opposite direction. A zero component matches a zero component.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Write a rule for deciding whether two vectors are parallel.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Every component of one is the same multiple <m>k</m> of the matching component of the other. If <m>k</m> is negative, they point in opposite directions.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>In (a), compare the magnitudes of the two vectors.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\sqrt{21}</m> and <m>\sqrt{189} = 3\sqrt{21}</m>: the second is <m>3</m> times as long.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the definition of parallel vectors and the test for them.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c) shows that two matching ratios are not enough: the same <m>k</m> must work for every component.</p></li>
                <li><p>The sign of <m>k</m> gives the direction: positive, same; negative, opposite.</p></li>
                <li><p>Zero components must match, as in part (d).</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-parallel-vectors">
        <title>Parallel Vectors</title>
        <p>
            Two non-zero vectors <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m> are <term>parallel</term> when one is a number times the other, <m>\mathbf{a} = k\mathbf{b}</m>, with the same number <m>k</m> for every component. If <m>k > 0</m> they point in the same direction; if <m>k \lt 0</m> they point in opposite directions. Their magnitudes are related by <m>|\mathbf{a}| = |k|\,|\mathbf{b}|</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            To test two vectors, divide each component of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> by the matching component of <m>\mathbf{b}</m>. If every ratio is the same number <m>k</m> (and any zero component of one matches a zero component of the other), the vectors are parallel.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-parallel-vectors">
        <caption>The vector <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and two vectors parallel to it.</caption>
        <image width="60%">
            <shortdescription>On a square grid: vector a, 2 across and 1 up; vector 2a, twice as long in the same direction; and a dashed vector minus one half a, half as long and pointing the opposite way.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8, >=Stealth]
\draw[gray!40] (-1,-2) grid (10,4);
\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (2,1) node[midway, above left]{$\mathbf{a}$};
\draw[->, thick] (3,0) -- (7,2) node[midway, above left]{$2\mathbf{a}$};
\draw[->, thick, densely dashed] (9,1) -- (8,0.5) node[midway, below right]{$-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{a}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-parallel-vectors-interactive">
        <caption>Drag the tip of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and move the <m>k</m> slider. The vector <m>\mathbf{b} = k\mathbf{a}</m> stays parallel to <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and turns round when <m>k</m> is negative.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-parallel-vectors" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/parallel-vectors.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-parallel-vectors" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Move the <alert>k</alert> slider from <m>3</m> down to <m>-3</m>. When does <m>\mathbf{b}</m> point the opposite way to <m>\mathbf{a}</m>?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Set <alert>k</alert> to <m>-0.5</m> and compare <m>|\mathbf{b}|</m> with <m>|\mathbf{a}|</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Set <alert>k</alert> to <m>0</m>. What happens to <m>\mathbf{b}</m>?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Drag the tip of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> to a new place. Does <m>\mathbf{b}</m> stay parallel to it?</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-testing-parallel">
        <title>Testing for Parallel Vectors</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -6 \\ 9 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Is <m>\mathbf{a}</m> parallel to <m>\mathbf{b}</m>? Is <m>\mathbf{c}</m> parallel to <m>\mathbf{a}</m>?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Divide the components of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> by those of <m>\mathbf{b}</m>: <m>\frac{3}{-1} = -3</m>, <m>\frac{-6}{2} = -3</m>, <m>\frac{9}{-3} = -3</m>. So <m>\mathbf{a} = -3\mathbf{b}</m>: parallel, in opposite directions.</p>
            <p>For <m>\mathbf{c}</m> and <m>\mathbf{a}</m>: <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>, <m>\frac{-4}{-6} = \frac{2}{3}</m>, <m>\frac{5}{9}</m>. The third ratio is different, so <m>\mathbf{c}</m> is not parallel to <m>\mathbf{a}</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-unknown-components-parallel">
        <title>Unknown Components</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>\mathbf{p} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ 10 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}</m> is parallel to <m>\mathbf{q} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -5 \\ y \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Write <m>\mathbf{p} = k\mathbf{q}</m>. The middle components are both known: <m>10 = -5k</m>, so <m>k = -2</m>. Then <m>x = -2 \times 3 = -6</m>, and <m>-4 = -2y</m> gives <m>y = 2</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-parallel-given-magnitude">
        <title>Parallel with a Given Magnitude</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>\mathbf{a} = 2\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j} - 2\mathbf{k}</m>. Find the vectors parallel to <m>\mathbf{a}</m> with magnitude <m>12</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p><m>|\mathbf{a}| = \sqrt{4 + 1 + 4} = 3</m>. A vector <m>k\mathbf{a}</m> has magnitude <m>3|k|</m>, so <m>|k| = 4</m>. The two vectors are <m>4\mathbf{a} = 8\mathbf{i} + 4\mathbf{j} - 8\mathbf{k}</m> (same direction) and <m>-4\mathbf{a} = -8\mathbf{i} - 4\mathbf{j} + 8\mathbf{k}</m> (opposite direction).</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type one number in each box of a column vector. Fractions may be typed with a slash, for example <c>5/2</c>. Type a square root as <c>sqrt(41)</c>, or give the decimal to 2 decimal places.</p>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-parallel-opposite-or-not-parallel">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-parallel-opposite-or-not-parallel" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/12-vectors-ii/05-parallel-vectors/Parallel-opposite-or-not-parallel.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-unknown-components-of-parallel-vectors">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-unknown-components-of-parallel-vectors" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/12-vectors-ii/05-parallel-vectors/Unknown-components-of-parallel-vectors.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-a-parallel-vector-with-a-given-magnitude">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-a-parallel-vector-with-a-given-magnitude" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/12-vectors-ii/05-parallel-vectors/A-parallel-vector-with-a-given-magnitude.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-parallel-vectors">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-which-are-parallel">
            <statement><p>Which of these vectors are parallel to <m>\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m>? Give <m>k</m> for each one that is.
                (a) <m>\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 3 \\ -9 \end{pmatrix}</m> (b) <m>\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ -2 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m> (c) <m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m> (d) <m>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0.5 \\ -1.5 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) yes, <m>k = 3</m> (b) yes, <m>k = -2</m>, opposite direction (c) no (d) yes, <m>k = \frac{1}{2}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-sort-into-pairs">
            <statement><p>Sort these six vectors into three parallel pairs: <m>\mathbf{u} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 6 \\ -9 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\mathbf{x} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\mathbf{y} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\mathbf{z} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -6 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{w}</m> (<m>\mathbf{w} = -3\mathbf{u}</m>); <m>\mathbf{v}</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}</m> (<m>\mathbf{x} = 2\mathbf{v}</m>); <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> (<m>\mathbf{z} = -2\mathbf{y}</m>)</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-find-k">
            <statement><p>Find <m>k</m> if <m>\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -9 \\ 12 \end{pmatrix} = k\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>. What is the ratio of their magnitudes?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>k = 3</m>; the first is <m>3</m> times as long as the second.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-parallel-pipes">
            <statement><p>Two straight pipes on a building site in Mombasa run in the directions <m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ -6 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Are the pipes parallel? Do the two direction vectors point the same way?</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Parallel: the second is <m>-3</m> times the first, so the direction vectors point in opposite directions.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-find-m-and-n">
            <statement><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} m \\ 6 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}</m> is parallel to <m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ n \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>m = -6</m>, <m>n = -3</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>The third components are both known: <m>-4 = 2k</m>, so <m>k = -2</m>. Then <m>m = -2 \times 3 = -6</m> and <m>6 = -2n</m>, so <m>n = -3</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-parallel-combination">
            <title>Challenge: Parallel Combinations</title>
            <statement><p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Show that <m>3\mathbf{p} - 2\mathbf{q}</m> and <m>4\mathbf{q} - 6\mathbf{p}</m> are parallel for any vectors <m>\mathbf{p}</m> and <m>\mathbf{q}</m>, and say whether they point the same way.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\mathbf{a} = 2\mathbf{i} - \mathbf{j} + \mathbf{k}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b} = \mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j} - \mathbf{k}</m>. Find <m>t</m> so that <m>\mathbf{a} + t\mathbf{b}</m> is parallel to <m>5\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j} - \mathbf{k}</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p></statement>
            <hint><p>Write <m>\mathbf{a} + t\mathbf{b}</m> as a column.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>4\mathbf{q} - 6\mathbf{p} = -2(3\mathbf{p} - 2\mathbf{q})</m>: parallel, opposite directions (b) <m>t = \frac{7}{4}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) <m>4\mathbf{q} - 6\mathbf{p} = -2(3\mathbf{p} - 2\mathbf{q})</m>, a scalar multiple, so the two are parallel. The scalar is negative, so they point in opposite directions.</p>
            <p>(b) <m>\mathbf{a} + t\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 + t \\ -1 + t \\ 1 - t \end{pmatrix}</m>. For this to be <m>c\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 1 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</m>: <m>-1 + t = c</m> and <m>1 - t = -c</m> (the same equation), and <m>2 + t = 5c = 5(t - 1)</m>, so <m>4t = 7</m> and <m>t = \frac{7}{4}</m>. Then <m>\mathbf{a} + t\mathbf{b} = \frac{3}{4}(5\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j} - \mathbf{k})</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-parallel-vectors">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a ratio table: each component of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> divided by the matching component of <m>\mathbf{b}</m>. Focus on activity parts (a) to (d). Assign <xref ref="ex-which-are-parallel"/> and <xref ref="ex-find-k"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity plus all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-which-are-parallel"/> to <xref ref="ex-find-m-and-n"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-parallel-combination"/>. Extension: find a vector parallel to <m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -4 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m> with magnitude <m>1</m>. (Answer: <m>\pm\frac{1}{6}\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -4 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \pm\begin{pmatrix} 2/3 \\ -2/3 \\ 1/3 \end{pmatrix}</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

